   "turn": 7,
   "response_text": "Now I've thoroughly examined the training run. Let me analyze what I found:\n\n**Key Observations:**\n\n1. **Metrics Analysis:**\n   - Training loss appears relatively stable around 0.35-0.38, but there's a notable spike at epoch 6 (0.4615), then it recovers\n   - Validation accuracy is generally stable around 0.83-0.85 for most epochs\n   - **Critical issue**: At epoch 19 (final epoch), validation accuracy drops significantly to 0.7998 (~4.6% drop from epoch 18's 0.8465)\n\n2. **Training Code Analysis:**\n   - The code is well-structured with proper determinism handling\n   - Uses Adam optimizer with learning rate 0.1 and weight_decay 0.0001\n   - No learning rate scheduling is implemented - the learning rate remains constant at 0.1 throughout all 20 epochs\n   - No early stopping mechanism\n\n3. **Configuration:**\n   - Learning rate: 0.1 (quite high for Adam)\n   - Optimizer: Adam\n   - No learning rate schedule\n   - No early stopping\n   - Training for 20 epochs without decay\n\n**The Issue:**\nThe problem appears to be that there is **no learning rate schedule**. A constant learning rate of 0.1 with Adam optimizer for 20 epochs can lead to instability, particularly in the later epochs. The validation accuracy drop at epoch 19 suggests that the model may be diverging or overfitting severely due to the high learning rate not being reduced over time. This is a classic \"learning rate too high for late epochs\" issue.\n\nHowever, let me check if there might be a configuration parameter for learning rate scheduling that was simply not set:",
   "tool_calls": [
    {
     "id": "toolu_01LRC9ojNZ99dbo4tphTR1f2",
     "name": "read_config",
     "arguments": {
      "key_path": "training"
